Mauvely OS: the login screen follows the device's people.json

The device's management agent writes /var/lib/mauvely-desktop/people.json:

  {"mode": "shared"|"assigned", "assigned": {"username", "name"}|null, "org": {"name", "slug"}}

  shared    No user tiles; the username field, as when the list is empty.
  assigned  The assigned person's tile, alone and preselected, even before they have
            an account on the device; "Other..." for anyone else, such as an admin.
  both      The organisation's name, quietly, under the clock.
  no file   The greeter as it was. So is a file that is not JSON or names another mode.

The greeter's C++ reads the file once, when it starts (DevicePeople). Only the greeter
narrows the list: UserModel::showOnly() is new, and the KCM never calls it. Who may sign
in is still PAM's decision; this only decides what the screen offers.

Against: plasma-login-manager 6.7.5 as Fedora f44 builds it. Independent of
0001-second-prompt.patch: either applies without the other.

`plasma-login-greeter --test` with PLASMALOGIN_MOCK_PEOPLE=<file> shows a mode without
PAM, and with no file in the variable, the greeter as it was.
